          "section:Common Pitfalls and How to Avoid Them"
        ],
        "exercise": "inventory",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "maintainability",
        "says": "Abstraction makes code easier to maintain and extend, since implementations can change behind a stable interface.",
        "basis": [
          "example:08-object-oriented/README_2.3.3.md:8"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "a claim about maintenance over time that no single test can observe; its concrete form is swapping processors in 'Payments through one processor interface'"
      },
      {
        "id": "overuse",
        "says": "Do not create an abstraction for every scenario, only where there is a clear need.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Common Pitfalls and How to Avoid Them"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "design judgement with no behaviour a test could check"
      },
      {
        "id": "small-interfaces",
        "says": "Design abstractions around behaviour, keep interfaces small (Interface Segregation) and prefer composition.",
